    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p4_b8",
      "text": "2.1\nSAM and Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        373.63,
        374.83,
        383.6
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        1494,
        1499,
        1534
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "SFBX1000",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 43,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p4_b9",
      "text": "SAM is a powerful prompt-based segmentation foundation model, but often re-\nquires adaptation for specialized downstream tasks [5]. Because full fine-tuning\nis costly in low-label regimes, parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods\nsuch as adapters [11], LoRA [13], and prompt tuning [19] are widely used.\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        392.42,
        480.56,
        438.25
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        1569,
        1922,
        1752
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "SFRM1000",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 309,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p4_b10",
      "text": "Prior work adapts SAM via lightweight modules or modified heads, e.g.,\nSAM-Adapter [4], Conv-Meets-LoRA [42], SU-SAM [32], and TS-SAM [40], while\nothers provide light semantic conditioning through text embeddings, e.g., SAM-\nPTx [14]. In contrast, we keep SAM\u2019s prompt encoder and mask decoder intact\nand inject CLIP-derived text, vision, and similarity features into the image en-\ncoder via semantic adapters, enabling text-prompted semantic conditioning with\nminimal architectural change.\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        440.24,
        480.59,
        521.94
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        1760,
        1922,
        2087
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "SFRM1000",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 490,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p4_b11",
      "text": "2.2\nVision\u2013Language Models for Semantic Segmentation\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        540.72,
        424.57,
        550.68
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        2162,
        1698,
        2202
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "SFBX1000",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 52,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p4_b12",
      "text": "Vision\u2013language models such as CLIP [28] align images and text in a shared\nembedding space and have been widely used for segmentation. Zero-shot and\nweakly supervised approaches derive activation maps or grouped regions from\nCLIP representations [3,6,10,26,30,34,43], but often suffer from coarse bound-\naries due to limited spatial precision [12].\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        559.51,
        480.6,
        617.29
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        2238,
        1922,
        2469
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "SFRM1000",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 348,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.24807_p4_b13",
      "text": "Open-vocabulary segmentation methods instead train large models with lan-\nguage supervision to generalize to unseen classes [7,9,20,23,35,44]. SemiVL [12]\nis an example that combines limited dense labels with a large unlabelled pool\nfor semi-supervised learning.\n",
      "bbox": [
        134.76,
        619.28,
        480.59,
        665.11
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        539,
        2477,
        1922,
